{"name":"Blue Raindrop","hex":"#c4d6e1","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"},{"term":"raindrop","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A single droplet of rainwater that has just fallen or is falling from the sky."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/raindrop"}],"entries":[{"title":"Drop (liquid)","blocks":[{"text":"A drop or droplet is a small column of liquid, bounded completely or almost completely by free surfaces. A drop may form when liquid accumulates at the end of a tube or other surface boundary, producing a hanging drop called a pendant drop. Drops may also be formed by the condensation of a vapor or by atomization of a larger mass of solid. Water vapor will condense into droplets depending on the temperature. The temperature at which droplets form is called the dew point."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/Water_drop_light_art_vijayanrajapuram_01.jpg/330px-Water_drop_light_art_vijayanrajapuram_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Drop_(liquid)","wikidata":"Q185789"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Water_drop_animation_enhanced_small.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Water_drop_animation_enhanced_small.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"A water drop falling from a tap","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWater_drop_animation_enhanced_small.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Bouncing_droplets_compact_size.gif/120px-Bouncing_droplets_compact_size.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/65/Bouncing_droplets_compact_size.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/65/Bouncing_droplets_compact_size.gif/120px-Bouncing_droplets_compact_size.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Drop of water bouncing on a water surface subject to vibrations","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABouncing_droplets_compact_size.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Drop_held_by_surface_tension.jpg/500px-Drop_held_by_surface_tension.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.o